WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:06.520
From sewing people together to chomping down
on a live octopus, these R-rated movies really

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.559
pushed audiences to their limits.

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:13.480
"Wolf Creek" is the product of unchecked misogyny
and a sickening fascination

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.610
with violence.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.880
The movie positions itself as a typical slasher
flick about three friends taking a trip through

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.240
the Australian outback when their car breaks
down in Wolf Creek Park.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.990
A man named Mick stumbles upon the group and
offers to help before revealing himself to

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:28.349
be a sadistic killer.

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:30.970
"That's not a knife.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.590
This is a knife."

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:38.590
The trio of protagonists in "Wolf Creek" contains
one man, but he's sidelined for almost the

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.700
entirety of the movie until he escapes with
his life at the end.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.079
Most of the screen time is dedicated to showing
Mick torturing the two women in ways that

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:49.789
the film seems to find entertaining.

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:54.829
After an implied sexual assault, Mick severs
limbs and spinal cords while stabbing and

00:00:54.829 --> 00:00:56.840
shooting to his heart's content.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.340
The movie never lets up on showing women in
horrendous pain and suffering.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.910
Roger Ebert rightfully called out the film
for its misogyny, writing,

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:10.330
"There is a role for violence in film, but
what the hell is the purpose of this sadistic

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:13.310
celebration of pain and cruelty?"

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:18.130
Slashers have their place, but they don't
have to be as openly hateful as "Wolf Creek."

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:22.450
Unfortunately the visceral nature of these
killings might be due to the movie being based

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:28.420
on a true story, that of Ivan Milat, the serial
killer known as the Backpacker Murderer.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.119
A movie with a title like "I Spit on Your
Grave" sets a certain tone for audiences before

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:34.619
they even walk into the theater.

00:01:34.619 --> 00:01:37.630
The film debuted in 1978 with a poster that
read,

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:44.100
"This woman has just cut, chopped, broken,
and burned five men beyond recognition...

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.420
but no jury in America would ever convict
her."

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.869
Aside from the dubious claim about juries,
the sentence perfectly sums up the film's

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:52.969
fascination with violence.

00:01:52.969 --> 00:01:56.930
Unfortunately, violence is really the only
thing the movie cares to show.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:01.530
The premise sees a woman seeking revenge on
five men after they sexually assault her,

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:07.278
and the weak plot merely serves as a means
to string together one horrifying scene after another.

00:02:07.278 --> 00:02:11.590
The assault that kicks off the movie's revenge
plot takes up a gratuitous amount of screen

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:16.510
time, and the brutal murders that follow it
don't really offer any sense of catharsis.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.090
Roger Ebert called the movie "a vile bag of
garbage" and continued by saying,

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.400
"Attending it was one of the most depressing
experiences of my life."

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.310
The shock factor can carry a movie pretty
far, though.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:34.190
Decades after its release, "I Spit on Your
Grave" somehow spawned a 2010 remake and multiple

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:37.440
sequels, each arguably as upsetting as the
original.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:43.060
"Sausage Party," the 2016 comedy from the
minds of Seth Rogen and Evan Goldberg begins

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.390
with a premise reminiscent of "Toy Story."

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:49.710
When the lights go out at a local grocery
store, the food items come alive.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:54.000
Most of them dream of one day being bought
by a customer and taken to the Great Beyond,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.450
but they don't realize they're destined to
be eaten alive.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:02.640
Feature-length adult animation with an imaginative
concept seems like a promising proposition.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:09.157
On release, the movie generated a ton of buzz,
and its critical reception was overwhelmingly positive.

00:03:09.157 --> 00:03:14.290
Looking back now, it's hard to imagine how
the film even earned a release, much less

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:19.580
critical praise, considering how heavily it
relies on being downright offensive for laughs.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:24.020
The crude sexual humor is rampant, but with
a name like "Sausage Party," what else would

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:25.020
you expect?

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.120
"You couldn't fit an eggplant in there!

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.569
It's too big, it'll tear you in half!"

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:33.700
However, what's really jarring is just how
many racial stereotypes were squeezed into

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:34.780
the film.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.090
Scene after scene pairs Kareem Abdul Lavash with Sammy Bagel

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:43.260
Jr. and forces them to play out one anti-Muslim
or anti-Semitic trope after another.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:47.760
A sentient bottle of Native American liquor
is given a cringe-worthy performance by Bill

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.840
Hader, and the movie's Twinkie character is
gay.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.709
The list of groups used as punch lines goes
on

00:03:53.709 --> 00:03:56.840
and on, but the movie, really, should not.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:01.689
"Bucky Larson: Born to Be a Star" really tries
its hardest to turn the adult film industry

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:06.450
into one interminable running joke, but it
really should have channeled its efforts elsewhere.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:11.786
The film follows its titular character after
he discovers that his parents used to be porn stars.

00:04:11.786 --> 00:04:16.820
Bucky takes their history as inspiration and
sets off for Hollywood, determined to find

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.979
his own way to break into the industry.

00:04:18.979 --> 00:04:20.660
"This is my destiny!

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.930
I was born to be a star!"

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:27.509
It doesn't take long for Bucky to realize
that his body doesn't exactly live up to adult

00:04:27.509 --> 00:04:32.440
film standards, but he finds his way to stardom
despite anything that he's lacking.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:36.770
The problem with "Bucky Larson" might not
be that it went too far with its lazy sex

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:41.040
humor, but that it didn't go far enough with
creating real characters or a story worth

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.479
paying attention to.

00:04:42.479 --> 00:04:48.180
There are only so many small penis jokes someone
can be expected to listen to, and "Bucky Larson"

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.509
fills out more than a lifetime's quota.

00:04:51.509 --> 00:04:54.920
Darren Aronofsky has become synonymous with
films that push boundaries.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:59.740
He isn't afraid to tell complicated stories
or to present audiences with horrifying imagery

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:03.200
that works to make the reality of his characters
really hit home.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.460
It's a filmmaking strategy that's definitely
earned him success, but one that's also sure

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.729
to turn away plenty of viewers.

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:14.600
"Requiem for a Dream" was Aronofsky's second
film, and he had ambitious plans for it.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:19.150
The movie is an adaptation of a Hubert Selby
Jr. novel, and the story centers on the lives

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.350
of a group of addicts living in New York City.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:25.370
Sara becomes addicted to amphetamines while
trying to lose weight for a potential television

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:29.500
appearance, while her son Harry and his friends
use and deal heroin.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:34.400
The film uses split screen segments and other
editing techniques to disorient audiences

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.919
and put them in the mindset of the movie's
fractured characters.

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:41.979
The bizarre and rapid editing is enough to
make someone feel a bit queasy in certain

00:05:41.979 --> 00:05:47.190
moments, but it's the movie's depiction of
needle injections, arm infections, violence,

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.520
and sexual assault that really takes things
over the top.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:55.610
The original cut of the film was rated NC-17,
but even though the R-rated version cut down

00:05:55.610 --> 00:06:01.880
on some of the sexual content, the rest is
still graphic enough to sicken an unprepared viewer.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.800
"Oldboy" is a 2003 South Korean film by director
Park Chan-wook.

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:11.490
Based on a Japanese manga of the same name,
the movie follows a man named Oh Dae-su who

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:16.280
is kidnapped and locked inside what amounts
to a hotel room for 15 years.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:21.360
When he's suddenly released, Oh Dae-su desperately
begins looking for the truth behind his imprisonment

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.520
and plotting his revenge against the people
who took him captive.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:26.880
By any measure, "Oldboy" is a fantastic film.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.370
It delivers a noir-styled story with some
truly memorable fight scenes and a plot that

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:33.580
keeps you guessing right up to the very end.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:40.000
However, critical acclaim and popular reception
aside, the movie still goes too far at least once.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.590
Some might find the big twist a bit on the
gross side, but that's not the gross-out moment

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:46.560
that puts "Oldboy" over the top.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:51.970
The octopus eating scene takes place shortly
after Oh Dae-su is released from his prison.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:56.699
He shows up at a sushi restaurant and demands
to eat something that's still alive.

00:06:56.699 --> 00:07:02.669
He proceeds to chow down on a live octopus,
which lead actor Choi Min-sik really did.

00:07:02.669 --> 00:07:08.060
Creating the scene took three takes and each
time Min-sik crushed a live octopus between

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.060
his teeth.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:12.610
Does the scene create a thematically resonant
moment in the movie?

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:13.610
Sure.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:15.120
Was it completely necessary?

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.250
Almost definitely not.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:22.210
The very premise of 2009's "The Human Centipede"
takes things so far that it's a wonder that

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.660
the movie ever got made.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:28.280
For anyone who's missed more than a decade's
worth of jokes about the film, here's a basic

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.460
breakdown of the plot.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:34.930
Dr. Josef Heiter is a retired surgeon-turned-psychopath
who's become obsessed with using his medical

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:37.240
skills to make obscene creatures.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:43.169
He kidnaps three tourists and sews them together
in a line to form one continuous digestive tract.

00:07:43.169 --> 00:07:47.259
After that, Heiter spends the rest of the
movie continuing to torment the three as he

00:07:47.259 --> 00:07:50.470
trains his "human centipede" as a pet.

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:55.133
Eventually, investigators stumble upon Heiter's
home and a bloodbath brings the movie to its conclusion.

00:07:55.133 --> 00:08:00.130
"The Human Centipede" is nauseating to even
think about, and watching it is no better.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:04.210
As we've already seen with other movies on
this list, though, nausea-inducing shocks

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:06.539
can take a movie pretty far.

00:08:06.539 --> 00:08:11.300
Horror movies often aim to make audiences
uncomfortable, but it's a thin line between

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:15.870
scaring an audience so badly they hide their
eyes and showing them something so repulsive

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.000
they're forced to look away from the screen.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.069
"Hostel: Part II" found the line, poked it,
then jumped right to the other side.

00:08:23.069 --> 00:08:27.069
The movie ran into problems well before it
even made its way into theaters.

00:08:27.069 --> 00:08:32.019
The original theatrical poster had to be banned
and replaced because it was too graphic.

00:08:32.019 --> 00:08:38.070
It simply featured a small bit of text overlaid
on an image of chopped-up, bloody flesh.

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:44.020
The replacement poster had an unclothed woman's
body holding what is presumably her own severed

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:47.800
head, so that one likely ran into issues at
most theaters as well.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:52.260
The story, such as it is, follows a group
of art students on vacation who unwittingly

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.510
get auctioned off to a secret ring of wealthy
torture fanatics.

00:08:56.510 --> 00:09:01.870
Scene after scene of nudity and torture play
out, including graphic depictions of scalping,

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:06.460
multiple beheadings, and severed genitalia
being fed to dogs.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:09.940
"Saw" took the world by storm in 2004.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:15.343
Horror fans were intrigued by the film's grotesque
premise and delighted by its sudden twist ending.

00:09:15.343 --> 00:09:20.269
A lot of the magic has to do with James Wan's
directing, which might be why there's little

00:09:20.269 --> 00:09:23.220
magic or substance to be found in the sequels.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:27.390
When you start with a sadistic killer dishing
out punishment via elaborate torture devices

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:32.000
to anyone he deems deserving, it's hard to
get much more disturbing.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.550
That didn't stop the "Saw" sequels from trying
to up the ante, and even though the

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:41.779
most squeamish of viewers stopped after the
first film, "Saw III" is the one that finally

00:09:41.779 --> 00:09:43.480
took things too far.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:48.587
There are, of course, all the nightmarish
traps that fans had come to expect from the franchise.

00:09:48.587 --> 00:09:53.260
A woman is ripped apart after fishing a key
out of acid and still failing to escape.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:58.060
Another is frozen to death, and a man is drowned
in the ground-up remains of pigs.

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:03.810
It's all nauseating stuff, but then the film
goes on to include a surgery scene in which

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:09.079
serial killer John Kramer has his skull drilled
open to relieve pressure on his brain.

00:10:09.079 --> 00:10:14.750
The movie is just one miserable scene after
another, presented with sloppy editing and

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:19.550
a story that would send an audience right
to sleep if it weren't for the constant onslaught

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.890
of nightmarish imagery on screen.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:25.970
A movie doesn't have to be gross or crude
to go too far.

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:31.500
Sometimes a film just finds a way to pour
gas on a fire that really needs to die down.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:37.279
In 1991, Oliver Stone released his political
thriller "JFK" starring Kevin Costner and

00:10:37.279 --> 00:10:38.279
Gary Oldman.

00:10:38.279 --> 00:10:42.370
The film begins with the investigation of
Kennedy's assassination, which ends after

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:44.310
Lee Harvey Oswald is killed.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:48.920
Years later, though, the investigation is
reopened and a conspiracy tying the murder

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:52.360
to members of the United States government
itself is uncovered.

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.770
"We're going back into the case Lou.

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:58.070
The murder of the president."

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:03.019
Conspiracy theories about the Kennedy assassination
have been running rampant since 1963, and

00:11:03.019 --> 00:11:08.440
the most diehard believers didn't need Stone
to step in and reaffirm their suspicions.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:14.120
He decided to do it anyway, and "JFK" glorifies
the kind of thinking that keeps new conspiracy

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:16.700
theories popping up online every day.

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:20.940
In case anyone thought that Stone had just
made the poor decision to use real conspiracy

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:25.640
theories as fodder for a big movie, he came
back and released a documentary called "JFK

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:28.550
Revisited: Through the Looking Glass" in 2021.

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:32.920
It takes the same ideas at the heart of the
original film's plot but presents them as

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:37.830
though they are definitive facts that Stone
himself has uncovered.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:42.740
Zack and Miri Make a Porno, like many raunchy
comedies, is actually a sweet movie when you

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:47.769
get right down to it — one about lifelong
best friends who may actually have romantic

00:11:47.769 --> 00:11:49.040
feelings for one another.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:53.880
However, the fact that it had the word "porno"
in its title meant the film already went way

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:55.700
too far for moviegoers.

00:11:55.700 --> 00:12:00.459
This was especially true in the movie's marketing,
which kicked off with a poster that proved

00:12:00.459 --> 00:12:05.520
so suggestive that it ended up getting banned
from theaters – and replaced with posters

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.400
that seemed barely related to the movie at
all.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:12.209
Afterwards, the feature encountered further
trouble running TV ads and public billboards

00:12:12.209 --> 00:12:17.100
with its full title, resulting in it being
given the generic moniker of "Zack and Miri"

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.660
on many promotional materials.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:23.640
Sometimes, a movie that stirs up this much
controversy can ride the publicity to box

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:26.500
office success – but that didn't happen
with this one.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.250
"If it's so easy, how come everybody doesn't
do it?"

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:31.089
"Because other people have options!

00:12:31.089 --> 00:12:32.089
And dignity!"

00:12:32.089 --> 00:12:36.150
Released at the very end of October 2008,
"Zack and Miri Make a Porno" ended up sinking

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:38.540
without a trace at the box office.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:43.170
The Kevin Smith comedy is ultimately a film
that wants to tug at your heartstrings, but

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:47.113
despite the racy title, it probably failed
to catch your attention.

